1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,756
One soft ball. Two hands. I can do this.
Oh! That was almost a catch. Dani, can

2
00:00:06,756 --> 00:00:08,346
we try that again?

3
00:00:08,346 --> 00:00:13,456
Absolutely. But first, I have a
challenge. Can you discover how your

4
00:00:13,456 --> 00:00:16,863
brain and body work together to catch
it?

5
00:00:16,863 --> 00:00:22,185
My hands are right here. The rest of the
team is a little harder to see.

6
00:00:25,185 --> 00:00:31,709
Welcome to our brain simulation. This
whole room is a model we can explore. We

7
00:00:31,709 --> 00:00:36,057
are not inside a real person, and nobody
has shrunk.

8
00:00:36,057 --> 00:00:41,943
Good. My shoes already fit. Let's find
the team before our next gentle toss.

9
00:00:41,943 --> 00:00:48,228
We will visit four outer lobes of the
cerebrum, the big, wrinkly part of the

10
00:00:48,228 --> 00:00:52,417
brain. Each side has these lobes. Other
parts help, too.

11
00:00:52,417 --> 00:00:57,899
Come with me. You can help spot the
clues. Watching is all you need to do.

12
00:00:57,899 --> 00:01:03,643
There is the ball in our replay. I
noticed its color, and I saw it coming

13
00:01:03,643 --> 00:01:04,361
toward me.

14
00:01:04,361 --> 00:01:10,218
Your eyes detect light. Information from
your eyes reaches your brain. Areas in

15
00:01:10,218 --> 00:01:16,074
the occipital lobe, toward the back,
help make sense of what you see.

16
00:01:16,074 --> 00:01:21,482
Occipital. That's a big name for a
helpful teammate. I'll remember the back

17
00:01:21,482 --> 00:01:22,924
of our brain map.

18
00:01:22,924 --> 00:01:27,843
Seeing involves other areas, too. This
highlight means we are looking here on

19
00:01:27,843 --> 00:01:33,141
our tour. It does not mean the rest of
the brain has switched off.

20
00:01:36,141 --> 00:01:42,042
Our first clue is seeing the ball. Which
lobe did we just visit that helps with

21
00:01:42,042 --> 00:01:45,411
seeing?

22
00:01:45,411 --> 00:01:51,343
The occipital lobe. If that name is new,
you can point to the back of our map.

23
00:01:51,343 --> 00:01:56,972
Now I can see the ball. But I still need
to work out where my hands should go.

24
00:01:56,972 --> 00:02:03,320
In the replay, my hands were a little
too far apart. The ball found the gap.

25
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:04,511
Very sneaky, ball.

26
00:02:04,511 --> 00:02:09,898
The parietal lobe helps process touch
and information about where your body

27
00:02:09,898 --> 00:02:16,183
is. Working with other areas, it helps
relate your hands to things around you.

28
00:02:16,183 --> 00:02:22,067
So the clue is not just ball over there.
It is ball over there, hands over here,

29
00:02:22,067 --> 00:02:23,797
and a gap between them.

30
00:02:26,797 --> 00:02:32,452
Exactly. Your body also sends
information back as you move. There is a

31
00:02:32,452 --> 00:02:34,627
lot of communication going on.

32
00:02:34,627 --> 00:02:40,580
Let's test our clue without throwing
anything. Watch the two hands on the

33
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:46,108
screen. First they're wide apart. Now
they move closer together. The ball

34
00:02:46,108 --> 00:02:50,785
stays in the same place while the space
around it changes.

35
00:02:52,785 --> 00:02:58,296
That comparison matters. Your brain
combines information about your body and

36
00:02:58,296 --> 00:03:05,309
the things nearby. It keeps updating as
you move, instead of saving one picture

37
00:03:05,309 --> 00:03:07,312
and hoping nothing changes.

38
00:03:07,312 --> 00:03:12,782
Like keeping a map up to date. The ball
has not moved, but the gap has.

39
00:03:12,782 --> 00:03:20,518
Which lobe helps us work out where our
hands are compared with the ball?

40
00:03:20,518 --> 00:03:25,363
The parietal lobe, working with its
teammates. You can find it toward the

41
00:03:25,363 --> 00:03:27,227
upper back of our map.

42
00:03:27,227 --> 00:03:33,278
I'm getting the picture. Seeing the ball
and knowing where my hands are have to

43
00:03:33,278 --> 00:03:35,547
work together. No more giant gap.

44
00:03:35,547 --> 00:03:41,600
Ready, Teo. Here comes the ball. That is
the cue you heard before our first toss.

45
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:47,596
I remember. That sound told me to pay
attention. I was ready in my head. My

46
00:03:47,596 --> 00:03:49,845
hands were still working on it.

47
00:03:49,845 --> 00:03:56,434
Areas in the temporal lobe, at the side
of the brain, help process sounds. Some

48
00:03:56,434 --> 00:04:01,265
parts here also help with memory. Other
regions join in, too.

49
00:04:01,265 --> 00:04:06,129
So my ears pick up sound, and my brain
helps me make sense of it.

50
00:04:06,129 --> 00:04:12,158
Yes. And you can catch a ball without
hearing this cue. Different situations

51
00:04:12,158 --> 00:04:13,550
use different information.

52
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:23,966
Which lobe on our map helps process the
sound of Dani speaking?

53
00:04:23,966 --> 00:04:29,289
The temporal lobe. Think of the side of
our map. You can use the label while you

54
00:04:29,289 --> 00:04:30,228
learn the name.

55
00:04:30,228 --> 00:04:34,970
Clue collected. Now for the plan: how do
I get my hands ready?

56
00:04:34,970 --> 00:04:42,121
My plan is to watch the ball and bring
my hands together where it is going. No

57
00:04:42,121 --> 00:04:45,066
dramatic diving. This is a gentle toss.

58
00:04:45,066 --> 00:04:50,642
Areas in the frontal lobe help plan
actions and control voluntary movements,

59
00:04:50,642 --> 00:04:53,430
the movements you choose to make.

60
00:04:53,430 --> 00:04:58,427
Like choosing to reach for the ball
instead of letting it visit the floor

61
00:04:58,427 --> 00:04:58,784
again.

62
00:04:58,784 --> 00:05:04,938
Right. The frontal lobe works with many
other areas. There is no tiny person in

63
00:05:04,938 --> 00:05:06,990
your brain giving every order.

64
00:05:09,990 --> 00:05:15,200
Which lobe at the front helps plan
actions and control the movements we

65
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:18,600
choose?

66
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:23,212
The frontal lobe. If you pointed to the
front of our map, you found it.

67
00:05:23,212 --> 00:05:29,710
Four lobes, four stops on our tour. But
our brain does not wait for a tour guide

68
00:05:29,710 --> 00:05:32,003
to finish talking before it works.

69
00:05:32,003 --> 00:05:38,013
Thankfully. These regions communicate
and cooperate. Our visits are not a list

70
00:05:38,013 --> 00:05:41,018
of steps every signal must follow.

71
00:05:41,018 --> 00:05:46,402
Wait. There is another part tucked
underneath at the back. We haven't met

72
00:05:46,402 --> 00:05:46,787
everybody.

73
00:05:46,787 --> 00:05:51,911
That is the cerebellum. It helps
coordinate movements and their timing.

74
00:05:51,911 --> 00:05:55,172
It also helps us learn from practice.

75
00:05:55,172 --> 00:06:00,223
That sounds useful for getting both
hands to the same appointment with the

76
00:06:00,223 --> 00:06:00,612
ball.

77
00:06:00,612 --> 00:06:06,030
Very useful. Movement signals travel
through the spinal cord and nerves to

78
00:06:06,030 --> 00:06:11,900
muscles in your arms and hands. Your
muscles pull to move your body.

79
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:20,563
And information from my body travels
back, too. This team keeps in touch.

80
00:06:20,563 --> 00:06:26,848
Yes. The brain and body work together.
The cerebellum is part of that team, not

81
00:06:26,848 --> 00:06:29,780
a guarantee that every catch will work.

82
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:36,315
Let's follow both directions on our
diagram. The gold path shows movement

83
00:06:36,315 --> 00:06:42,346
signals going toward the arm. The green
path shows information coming back.

84
00:06:44,346 --> 00:06:50,581
That return information helps the
nervous system keep track of what is

85
00:06:50,581 --> 00:06:56,297
happening. These lines are teaching
symbols, not two glowing strings inside

86
00:06:56,297 --> 00:07:00,454
you. Real nerves contain many fibers
carrying signals.

87
00:07:00,454 --> 00:07:06,560
So catching is not just sending one
command and walking away. The team keeps

88
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:07,433
checking in.

89
00:07:07,433 --> 00:07:13,060
Exactly. Practice gives that team more
chances to learn how a movement went.

90
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:20,582
Which part we just met helps coordinate
movements and their timing?

91
00:07:20,582 --> 00:07:25,990
The cerebellum. It is the smaller,
folded part at the lower back of our

92
00:07:25,990 --> 00:07:26,762
brain model.

93
00:07:26,762 --> 00:07:32,780
Missing one catch does not mean the team
is broken. We can learn with practice.

94
00:07:32,780 --> 00:07:37,237
Our map is getting busy. Who's waving
from that little screen?

95
00:07:37,237 --> 00:07:43,121
Three guide programs, based on real
kinds of brain cells. Real cells do not

96
00:07:43,121 --> 00:07:47,744
talk. We have enlarged these models so
we can meet them.

97
00:07:47,744 --> 00:07:53,244
I represent a neuron. Neurons send
messages using electrical and chemical

98
00:07:53,244 --> 00:07:56,744
signals. Lots of us connect into
networks.

99
00:07:56,744 --> 00:08:02,660
A whole team of tiny message senders.
Now the connections on our map make

100
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:03,082
sense.

101
00:08:03,082 --> 00:08:12,235
I represent an astrocyte. Astrocytes
support neurons and help keep conditions

102
00:08:12,235 --> 00:08:15,563
around them working well.

103
00:08:15,563 --> 00:08:22,941
I represent a microglial cell. Microglia
are immune cells. We help watch for

104
00:08:22,941 --> 00:08:26,347
trouble and clear away damaged material.

105
00:08:29,347 --> 00:08:32,287
So you do not rush in and fix a missed
catch.

106
00:08:32,287 --> 00:08:37,823
No. Clearing cellular debris is a
different job. Your practice catch

107
00:08:37,823 --> 00:08:40,339
belongs to the movement team.

108
00:08:40,339 --> 00:08:45,157
These are just three kinds of cells.
There are many more teammates to

109
00:08:45,157 --> 00:08:45,528
discover.

110
00:08:45,528 --> 00:08:51,563
I'm ready for another gentle try. Ball,
please keep your appointment with my

111
00:08:51,563 --> 00:08:51,994
hands.

112
00:08:51,994 --> 00:08:53,381
Here it comes.

113
00:08:56,381 --> 00:09:01,581
Nice catch, Teo. And if it had fallen,
we could simply try again.

114
00:09:01,581 --> 00:09:07,571
We saw the ball, worked out where it
was, planned a movement, and coordinated

115
00:09:07,571 --> 00:09:11,849
our hands. My nerves and muscles helped
make it happen.

116
00:09:11,849 --> 00:09:17,676
And you heard my cue. Different brain
regions and your body were working

117
00:09:17,676 --> 00:09:20,365
together, with information moving
between them.

118
00:09:20,365 --> 00:09:28,911
Did one part make that catch happen, or
did lots of parts work together?

119
00:09:28,911 --> 00:09:34,298
Lots of parts, working together. That is
our brain team challenge solved.

120
00:09:34,298 --> 00:09:40,849
One catch. A whole team. You can keep
being a brain detective just by watching

121
00:09:40,849 --> 00:09:41,722
everyday actions.

122
00:09:41,722 --> 00:09:48,258
If you try a gentle soft-ball catch
later, ask a trusted grown-up and choose

123
00:09:48,258 --> 00:09:51,118
a clear space. Watching is fine, too.

124
00:09:51,118 --> 00:09:55,969
Thanks for exploring with me. Next time,
another brain mystery.
